        </nav>
        <p class="eyebrow"><span></span> Made by one person</p>
        <h1 itemprop="headline">I built the tracker I wanted to use.</h1>
        <p itemprop="description">WeekScale is built by one person. It exists because I wanted a weight tracker that was genuinely focused on the weekly format, and I could not find one that was that ticked all the boxes I cared about.</p>
    </header>

    <div class="guide-layout shell">
        <nav class="contents-nav guide-nav" aria-label="Article sections">
            <span>In this story</span>
            <a href="#why">Why I built it</a>
            <a href="#values">The values</a>
            <a href="#reward">The only thing it rewards</a>
            <a href="#roadmap">What's next</a>
        </nav>

        <div class="guide-content" itemprop="articleBody">
            <section class="prose-section" id="why">
                <p class="guide-kicker">The short story</p>
                <p>I wanted a weight tracker that was hyper-focused on the weekly format: no rolling averages, no dashboard stuffed with features I never used, no ads, no subscription nagging, and no forced account just to use my own data.</p>
                <p>When I could not find that, I built it. If you are here for the same reasons, this is probably the tracker you were looking for too.</p>
                <div class="guide-principle">
                    <strong>WeekScale stays simple because a weekly average is already the whole idea.</strong>
                </div>
            </section>

            <section class="prose-section" id="values">
                <p class="guide-kicker">What WeekScale refuses to be</p>
                <p>Those are the values I look for in an app I use myself. I believe in them, so WeekScale is built around them rather than around growth tactics.</p>
                <ul class="check-list">
                    <li>No subscription &mdash; pay once, keep updates</li>
                    <li>No ads, no analytics, no AI gimmicks</li>
                    <li>No account &mdash; data stays on your device</li>
                    <li>No data sharing</li>
                </ul>
            </section>

            <section class="prose-section" id="reward">
                <p class="guide-kicker">The only thing WeekScale rewards</p>
                <p>WeekScale never judges the number itself. There are no badges for losing weight, no celebrations for reaching a target, and no guilt for gaining. The only thing it positively reinforces is showing up: how many times you weigh in during a week.</p>
                <p>Get five or more weigh-ins in a calendar week and the week earns its green card &mdash; the same "reliable-week treatment" behind the Reliability Score. That is the whole prize, and it is a prize about the quality of your data, not the number on the scale.</p>
                <div class="guide-principle">
                    <strong>The green card is the only prize.</strong>
                    <span>Five or more weigh-ins make a week complete enough to trust. Nothing else in WeekScale is rewarded.</span>
                </div>
            </section>

            <section class="prose-section" id="roadmap">
                <p class="guide-kicker">The plan</p>
                <p>WeekScale is a solo project, so the scope stays honest. The roadmap is shaped by beta feedback and is mostly UI-focused right now: refining the weekly view, navigation, and the small details that make it calm to use.</p>
                <p>From there, the plan is to sync weigh-ins from Apple Health on iOS and Google Health Connect on Android, depending on the platform.</p>
                <p>One thing that will never be on the roadmap is a goal weight. I don't want WeekScale to own any part of deciding what a "good" number is for your body &mdash; setting one is your call alone, and I won't carry shared responsibility for a target someone sets too low or too high.</p>
                <div class="detail-list">
                    <div><strong>Now</strong><span>UI-focused refinements</span></div>
                    <div><strong>Next</strong><span>Apple Health on iOS</span></div>
                    <div><strong>Next</strong><span>Health Connect on Android</span></div>
                </div>
                <div class="guide-principle">
                    <strong>Sync stays on your device.</strong>
                    <span>Health data is read locally when you choose to import it &mdash; nothing is uploaded.</span>
                </div>
                <p>Questions, ideas, or feedback? Send them through the <a href="/support">support form</a>. Every message helps shape what comes next.</p>
            </section>

            <section class="guide-cta">
                <p class="eyebrow"><span></span> Join the journey</p>
                <h2>Your week is the unit.</h2>
                <p>Help shape WeekScale by joining the beta and telling me what you would like to see.</p>
                <a class="button button-primary" href="/#beta">Join the WeekScale beta</a>
            </section>
        </div>
